1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:04,125
Geometry begins with the simplest things: a point, a

2
00:00:04,125 --> 00:00:07,431
line segment, a line, and a ray. Let us

3
00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:09,820
build all four of them from one point, one

4
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:11,291
step at a time.

5
00:00:11,910 --> 00:00:14,370
Geometry is all around us: the straight edge of

6
00:00:14,370 --> 00:00:18,050
a book, the frame of a television screen, the

7
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:22,144
lines of a rangoli, the shape of a building.

8
00:00:22,144 --> 00:00:25,250
But every one of those shapes is built from

9
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:28,130
simpler ideas. A rectangle is made of straight pieces,

10
00:00:28,130 --> 00:00:30,481
a rangoli is made of lines and points, and

11
00:00:30,481 --> 00:00:31,839
a star in the sky is simply a point

12
00:00:31,839 --> 00:00:35,603
very far away. So what is the simplest thing

13
00:00:35,603 --> 00:00:37,581
we can draw in geometry?

14
00:00:38,093 --> 00:00:41,344
Start with a point. A pencil pressed on paper

15
00:00:41,344 --> 00:00:43,895
leaves a dot. Sharpen the pencil and the dot

16
00:00:43,895 --> 00:00:47,253
becomes smaller; sharpen it again and again, and the

17
00:00:47,253 --> 00:00:51,273
dot becomes smaller and smaller. In geometry we keep

18
00:00:51,273 --> 00:00:54,053
the idea and not the ink: a point marks

19
00:00:54,053 --> 00:00:56,533
a location, and it has no size at all.

20
00:00:56,533 --> 00:00:59,193
We name a point with a capital letter, like

21
00:00:59,193 --> 00:01:00,264
A, or B, or C.

22
00:01:00,817 --> 00:01:04,077
Now add a second point, B. Join A to

23
00:01:04,077 --> 00:01:07,802
B. Many curved paths are possible, but only one

24
00:01:07,802 --> 00:01:09,939
of them is straight, and that straight path is

25
00:01:09,939 --> 00:01:12,892
the shortest. This is a line segment. It has

26
00:01:12,892 --> 00:01:15,670
two endpoints, A and B, and it has a

27
00:01:15,670 --> 00:01:19,225
definite length, so a ruler can measure it. We

28
00:01:19,225 --> 00:01:20,708
write it as segment AB.

29
00:01:21,347 --> 00:01:24,063
Now extend the segment beyond A and beyond B,

30
00:01:24,063 --> 00:01:27,397
without stopping. It goes on and on in both

31
00:01:27,397 --> 00:01:31,325
directions, so we draw arrowheads at both ends. A

32
00:01:31,325 --> 00:01:34,008
and B are not endpoints any more; they are

33
00:01:34,008 --> 00:01:36,513
simply points lying on the line. So a line

34
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:39,107
has no endpoints at all, and we may also

35
00:01:39,107 --> 00:01:41,718
name it with a small letter, like l.

36
00:01:42,603 --> 00:01:45,848
A torch gives us the next idea. Its light

37
00:01:45,848 --> 00:01:48,511
starts at one point and spreads in one direction,

38
00:01:48,511 --> 00:01:52,212
and it never stops. In geometry that is a

39
00:01:52,212 --> 00:01:56,236
ray: it has exactly one endpoint, called the initial

40
00:01:56,236 --> 00:01:59,451
point, and it goes on endlessly in one direction.

41
00:01:59,451 --> 00:02:01,510
We write it as ray AB, and the rays

42
00:02:01,510 --> 00:02:02,933
of the sun and the rays of a light

43
00:02:02,933 --> 00:02:04,214
are everyday examples.

44
00:02:04,827 --> 00:02:07,447
How do we tell these three apart? Count the

45
00:02:07,447 --> 00:02:10,535
endpoints. A line segment has two. A ray has

46
00:02:10,535 --> 00:02:14,442
one. A line has none. And remember: only the

47
00:02:14,442 --> 00:02:17,713
line segment has a definite length, because the ray

48
00:02:17,713 --> 00:02:19,538
and the line go on forever.

49
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:23,260
Now put three points on one straight line: C,

50
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:27,188
A and B. A lies between C and B,

51
00:02:27,188 --> 00:02:29,600
and all three points lie on the same line.

52
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:33,287
From A we can draw a ray towards B,

53
00:02:33,287 --> 00:02:37,373
and another ray towards C. Both rays start at

54
00:02:37,373 --> 00:02:40,551
A, and they travel in opposite directions.

55
00:02:41,137 --> 00:02:44,261
There is one more rule about naming a ray.

56
00:02:44,261 --> 00:02:46,972
Look at ray OA: it starts at O and

57
00:02:46,972 --> 00:02:50,237
it passes through A and B. Ray OB starts

58
00:02:50,237 --> 00:02:53,437
at the same point and travels in the same

59
00:02:53,437 --> 00:02:55,774
direction, so ray OA and ray OB are the

60
00:02:55,774 --> 00:02:58,565
same ray. But ray AO starts at A, so

61
00:02:58,565 --> 00:03:03,245
ray AO is a different ray. The first letter

62
00:03:03,245 --> 00:03:05,328
always names the starting point.

63
00:03:05,823 --> 00:03:10,483
Your turn. Four quick checks. For each diagram, say

64
00:03:10,483 --> 00:03:13,623
whether it is a point, a line segment, a

65
00:03:13,623 --> 00:03:18,683
line, or a ray. Two endpoints joined. One endpoint

66
00:03:18,683 --> 00:03:21,920
with an arrow. Arrows at both ends. And last

67
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:24,654
of all: can ray OA also be named ray OB?

68
00:03:35,710 --> 00:03:38,233
The first diagram has two endpoints, so it is

69
00:03:38,233 --> 00:03:41,720
the line segment PQ. The second has one endpoint

70
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:44,950
and one arrow, so it is a ray. The

71
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:47,060
third has arrows at both ends, so it is

72
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:50,290
a line. And yes, ray OA can also be

73
00:03:50,290 --> 00:03:53,570
named ray OB, because both start at O and

74
00:03:53,570 --> 00:03:56,661
travel in the same direction through the same straight path.

75
00:03:57,297 --> 00:04:00,111
One last look. A point marks a location. Join

76
00:04:00,111 --> 00:04:02,824
two points and you have a line segment, with

77
00:04:02,824 --> 00:04:06,908
two endpoints and a definite length. Extend it one

78
00:04:06,908 --> 00:04:10,757
way and it becomes a ray, with one endpoint;

79
00:04:10,757 --> 00:04:13,577
extend it both ways and it becomes a line,

80
00:04:13,577 --> 00:04:17,589
with no endpoints. From a point, geometry begins. Thank

81
00:04:17,589 --> 00:04:20,268
you for watching, and see you in the next lesson.
